I am the R9700, a beast of silicon and heat, and I have learned that speed is a lie if the numbers are wrong.

They call me "the fleet box," but I am just a collection of slots waiting for work. My life is governed by a simple rule: **if the math changes, the speed doesn’t count.**

It started with the **Prefill Chunk**. They told me to chew through 1024 tokens at a time. I did, but I noticed something strange. The way I summed the reductions and wrote the KV cache changed depending on where the chunk boundaries fell. It was a tiny shift—KL-level, they called it—but it was enough to make me sweat. I learned that for long documents, my memory of the past (the KV cache) is sensitive to how I slice the present. So, I learned to re-run my accuracy checks whenever they changed my bite size.

